{{nihongo|'''Anjō Motoyasu'''|安条元康||extra=15 July 1576 - 22 March 1643}} was a prominent Sanese samurai who lived during the Sengoku and Seto-Tosa periods. Born in the Mikata Domain, Okaiken, Motoyasu was the eldest son of Anjō Motonobu, a respected daimyō who ruled over the territory of Mikata. Following his father's untimely demise in the Battle of Saikawa, Motoyasu, at the age of 14, assumed leadership over his father's clan.

==Early life==

Motoyasu was born on July 15, 1576, in the Mikata Domain of Okaiken. He was the eldest son of Anjō Motonobu, a prominent daimyō who ruled over a territory in Mikata. Motoyasu grew up in a household that valued of martial arts and military strategy.  His father recognized his potential and ensured that he received education from his retainers. Motoyasu learned various martial skills and developed a deep understanding of military strategy. He spent countless hours practicing swordsmanship, archery, and horsemanship, striving to excel in every aspect. In addition to physical training, Motoyasu also received a comprehensive education, studying historical battles and tactical treatises that shaped his strategic thinking. From an early age, he was trained by his father Motonobu and by the age of . 

Motoyasu's early years were not solely focused on martial arts. The turbulent political climate and constant warfare served as the backdrop to his formative years, imprinting upon him the importance of strength, resilience, and adaptability. His early exposure to martial arts, military strategy shaped his character and prepared him for the responsibilities as a samurai. These formative experiences would prove instrumental in his later military career, where he would rise to become one of the most respected and accomplished warriors of his time.

==Military career==

Anjō Motoyasu swiftly emerged as a formidable warrior and a gifted strategist. He participated in numerous battles during the Sengoku Period, earning praise from his superiors for his unwavering bravery and exceptional skill on the battlefield.

One of Motoyasu's most remarkable victories came during the Invasion of Yagikoshu in 1592. Despite facing overwhelming odds against a much larger Uesugi army, Motoyasu defied expectations and led a small force of samurai to triumph. Employing a combination of surprise attacks and ingenious tactics, Motoyasu orchestrated a devastating ambush. By strategically positioning his men in the trees and bushes near the Uesugi camp, his forces caught the enemy off guard and swiftly turned the tide of battle, emerging victorious. Motoyasu recognized the challenges ahead and meticulously planned his strategy for the invasion. He assembled a diverse and well-trained force, comprising experienced samurai, foot soldiers, and archers. Understanding the importance of intelligence gathering, he dispatched scouts to gather information about the Uesugi clan's troop movements, fortifications, and supply lines. This information would prove crucial in the execution of his strategy.

One of Motoyasu's key strengths as a military commander was his ability to adapt and innovate. He understood that a direct confrontation with the larger and more established Uesugi army would be disadvantageous. Instead, he devised a plan that leveraged guerrilla tactics, surprise attacks, and diversionary maneuvers to weaken the Uesugi forces and exploit their vulnerabilities.

The invasion began with a series of lightning-fast raids and skirmishes, aimed at disrupting Uesugi supply lines and sowing confusion among their ranks. Motoyasu's forces targeted key logistical points, such as granaries and armories, while avoiding direct confrontations with the main Uesugi army. These hit-and-run tactics proved highly effective, inflicting significant losses on the Uesugi forces and weakening their morale. Motoyasu's ability to coordinate his troops and maintain their morale under challenging circumstances was a testament to his leadership skills. He instilled a sense of discipline and loyalty among his men, fostering a cohesive and determined fighting force.

The decisive moment of the Second Invasion of Yagikoshu came in the Second Battle of Saikawa. Motoyasu identified Saikawa Castle as a crucial stronghold controlled by the Uesugi forces, serving as a linchpin in their defensive strategy. Capturing Saikawa Castle would not only weaken the Uesugi clan's grip on the region but also provide a strategic advantage for the Honda forces. Motoyasu devised a multifaceted plan to capture Saikawa Castle. He initiated a two-pronged attack, with one force launching a frontal assault on the castle gates, while another group conducted a diversionary attack on the Uesugi rear. The diversionary attack succeeded in drawing a significant portion of the Uesugi forces away from the main battlefront, creating an opportunity for the Honda forces to breach the castle defenses.

The Battle of Saikawa was intense and hard-fought, with both sides displaying remarkable resilience and determination. Motoyasu led his troops from the frontlines, rallying them with his unwavering courage and strategic acumen. Despite facing staunch resistance, the Honda forces gradually gained the upper hand, overwhelming the Uesugi defenders with their coordinated assaults and superior tactics. With the fall of Saikawa Castle, The Anjō clan was finally able to take over Northern Yagikoshu and avenge the disastrous First Invasion of Yagikoshu.

In the Battle of Otarai in 1600, Motoyasu demonstrated his prowess once again. Leading a combined force of Okaiken and Honda samurai against a sizable army of Takigawa loyalists, he engaged in a fiercely contested conflict. Employing a blend of surprise attacks, guerrilla warfare, and superior tactical maneuvers, Motoyasu secured a hard-fought victory. This triumph played a crucial role in securing overall victory for the Honda clan in the Battle of Tone, a turning point that marked the end of the Sengoku Period.

Following the pivotal Battle of Sekigahara, Motoyasu assumed the role of a diplomat, representing Honda Shigetada's foreign affairs. With his astute diplomatic skills, he successfully fostered and maintained amicable relations between Okaiken and other nations, solidifying the clan's position on the regional stage.

Motoyasu's military career showcased his remarkable abilities as a skilled warrior and a talented strategist. He remained a loyal retainer to Honda Shigetada, albeit with shades of complexity. Ambitious and driven, Motoyasu was known to make choices that aligned with his personal interests, even if it meant betraying his allies.

==Administrative career==

After the Battle of Tone, Motoyasu turned his attention to the task of rebuilding and revitalizing his clan's territory. Gifted with administrative acumen, he set about restoring the fortunes of the Anjō clan. His capable leadership allowed him to implement measures that improved the lives of his subjects, earning him a reputation for fairness and justice.

==Later Life and Legacy==

Motoyasu continued to serve as a daimyō until his passing in 1643. Throughout his life, he garnered widespread respect as a revered leader and a skilled warrior. His enduring influence solidified his position as one of the pivotal figures in the annals of Okaiken's history.

Anjō Motoyasu's legacy is one marked by his exceptional military achievements, strategic acumen, and unwavering loyalty. His pivotal role in expanding the territorial influence of the Anjō clan was matched only by his diplomatic endeavors, which contributed significantly to fostering cordial relations with neighboring lands.

==Personal life==

Motoyasu's personal life was marked by significant relationships and familial connections that played a role in shaping his experiences and decisions.

Motoyasu was married twice in his lifetime. His first wife was Oyui, the daughter of Fukumoto Motonari, a powerful daimyō from a neighboring domain. The marriage was primarily arranged for political and strategic reasons, aiming to strengthen alliances between the Anjō and Fukumoto clans. Despite the union, Motoyasu and Oyui had no children. It is said that they had a cordial relationship, but their marriage lacked the emotional depth and companionship that Motoyasu longed for. 

Motoyasu entered into a second marriage with Masako, the younger sister of Honda Shigetomo, a prominent daimyō and Motoyasu's close ally. Masako was known for her beauty, intelligence, and strong character. Their marriage proved to be a fruitful one, as they had 3 sons together: Motokatsu, who would later inherit Motoyasu's legacy; Motoshige, who would establish a branch of the Anjō clan in Saikawa; and Motosada, who was killed in the Battle of Ichijōdani.

Despite the demands of his military career and the tumultuous times he lived in, Motoyasu cherished his family and sought solace and support in their presence. He valued the stability and comfort that his family provided, offering him a sense of purpose beyond the battlefield. Motoyasu's relationship with his son, Motokatsu, was particularly important to him, as he saw in him the potential to carry on the family name and continue the Anjō lineage.

Beyond his immediate family, Motoyasu also maintained close relationships with his comrades and fellow warriors. Motoyasu fostered deep bonds of trust and respect with his retainers. He was known to be a fair and just leader, taking care of his subordinates and valuing their contributions to his military endeavors. Motoyasu sought a balance between his duties as a daimyō and the desire for companionship and family. While the demands of his military career often took precedence, he valued the support and stability that his loved ones provided. These personal relationships and connections served as an anchor for Motoyasu, offering him solace and motivation amidst the tumultuous times he lived in.

==Appraisal==

Anjō Motoyasu was a multifaceted and enigmatic figure. While admired for his remarkable military prowess, strategic brilliance, and loyalty to his lord, he possessed traits that were both admirable and contentious. His ambition and ruthlessness, juxtaposed with his sense of duty and dedication, added complexity to his character. Despite these contradictions, Motoyasu left an indelible mark on the history of Okaiken, instrumental in shaping its destiny and earning his place as one of its most esteemed figures.

